# Testing Evidence — Reflex

Integration testing performed against the real database and API, not
mocked. All results below are from an actual live run.

## Setup verified

- Ran `database/schema.sql` against a fresh MySQL/MariaDB instance —
  completed with zero errors, created 8 tables.
- Ran `database/seed.sql` — completed with zero errors, populated users,
  retailers, products, and sample deliveries.
- Started `server/server.js` and confirmed `/api/health` returns
  `{"status":"ok","database":"connected"}`.

## Core workflow — retailer → dispatcher → rider

| Step | Action | Result |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | Retailer creates a delivery request (`POST /api/deliveries`) | `201` — delivery created with status `REQUESTED` |
| 2 | Dispatcher views open deliveries (`GET /api/deliveries`) | New request visible in the list |
| 3 | Dispatcher assigns it to a rider (`POST /api/deliveries/:id/assign`) | `201` — status moved to `ASSIGNED` |
| 4 | Rider updates status to `PICKED_UP` (`POST /api/deliveries/:id/status`) | `201` — status updated |
| 5 | Full history retrieved (`GET /api/deliveries/:id/history`) | Correctly shows all 3 status changes with timestamps and who made each one |

Result: **the complete core workflow works correctly end-to-end.**

## Edge cases tested

| Case | Expected behavior | Actual result |
|---|---|---|
| Assign a delivery that's already assigned to someone | Should be rejected | Rejected with `409 Conflict` — "Delivery is not available for assignment" |
| Rider tries to move status backward (e.g. `PICKED_UP` → `REQUESTED`) | Should be rejected | Rejected with `403 Forbidden` — "User cannot apply this status update" |
| Rider tries to update status after delivery is already `DELIVERED` | Should be rejected | Rejected with `403 Forbidden` |
| Rider scans to confirm delivery (`POST /api/deliveries/:id/confirm`) | Only the assigned rider can confirm | Succeeded (`201`) when called by the correct assigned rider |

Result: **duplicate-assignment protection and status-transition rules both
hold correctly under direct testing, not just in theory.**

## Issue found and fixed during testing

The database setup instructions did not account for a common MySQL/MariaDB
default: the `root` account is often restricted to local-socket-only
login, while the API connects over TCP (`127.0.0.1`). Following the
original README exactly produces `Access denied for user 'root'`. Fix: a
dedicated application database user must be created with TCP access
explicitly granted. This has been corrected in the updated README (see
below) so the next person to set this up doesn't hit the same wall.

